.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 110 PM PDT Fri Oct 9 2026
(This evening through Saturday night)

As of 1 PM PDT, temperatures are running between 10-25 degrees
cooler than this time yesterday with up to 30 degrees cooler in
isolated locations. Needless to say, the cool down has arrived.
Early morning stratus was slow to retreat to the coast, but with
mostly sunny skies across the interior we are expected this
afternoon to still be warm.

An upper level cut-off low pressure is approximately 400 miles
southwest of the Monterey Bay. This has allowed for a deepening
marine layer that is forecast to deepen further tonight and into
Saturday morning to around 2000 feet. As such, have added coastal
drizzle into the forecast south of the Golden Gate where there is
about a 20-60% probability (based off of the HREF) of seeing greater
than 0.01" of precipitation from 8 PM this evening through 8 AM
Saturday. This is a typical pattern for which we see widespread
coastal drizzle!

By late tonight and into Saturday morning, a mostly dry frontal
passage (minus the drizzle) is forecast to move from north the south
across the Bay Area and Central Coast. This will bring temperatures
down to below seasonal averages Saturday, generally 5 to 15 degrees
cooler. In wake of the frontal passage, winds will increase out of
the northwest with afternoon and evening gusts between 35-45 mph in
the favored gaps, passes, and wind-prone areas. Winds over the ocean
will be even stronger with gale-force gusts likely!

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 110 PM PDT Fri Oct 9 2026
(Sunday through next Thursday)

Winds ease a bit on Sunday with an even colder air mass settling
into the region from the north. Temperatures will struggle to warm
into the 60s to lower 70s across much of the region. By Sunday night
into Monday, there is a chance for rain showers over the north Central
Coast as mid/upper level remnant moisture from Tropical Storm
Rachel and an upper level cut-off low move across the region.
Across the Bay Area and North Bay, there is a less than 10-15%
chance of precipitation. This could change depending on the track
of the cut- off low and remnant moisture.

Cooler conditions persist into midweek before a gradual warming
trend takes palace towards the latter half of the week as ridging
builds in from the eastern Pacific.

